  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 213 PM CDT Wed Jul 29 2026\n\nThis afternoon, meridional flow continues across the CONUS with a \ndeep trough axis across the northeast and an expansive ridge axis \nover the Desert Southwest and High Plains. A shortwave is noted on \nwater vapor imagery over western KS on the northeast portion of the \nmain mid-level ridge. Associated lift ahead of the shortwave in a \nunstable and very moist environment (pwats currently around 2.2 \ninches) is leading to additional shower/t-storm development this \nafternoon in central KS. That rain will slowly move east and is\nexpected to dissipate with time this evening. Another round of rain \nis looking possible (40-60% chance) tonight and early Thursday, with  \nanother shortwave in the west-northwest flow combined with \nisentropic ascent. Severe storms are not expected, however, isolated\nheavy rainfall could again occur given the very high pwats and \noverall weak atmospheric flow.\n\nA stronger wave will round the northern portion of the ridge across \nthe High Plains and into the Midwest tomorrow. That will bring a \ncold front into and through the area. While CAMs differ slightly in \nlocation, timing and coverage, most agree that thunderstorms will \ndevelop near the boundary Thursday night. Better forcing and a \nhigher amount of shear could allow for a few strong to severe \nstorms, with damaging wind still looking like the primary hazard. \nHeavy rain could also occur with pwats remaining very high ahead of \nthe front, but storms should progress eastward with the boundary, \nhelping to limit the timeframe of heavy rain at any one location. \nClouds will clear throughout the day on Friday with drier (lower \nTd's) air eventually working into the area.\n\nA surface ridge will expand over the region this weekend. Pleasant \nconditions are expected Saturday with sunshine, lower dew points in\nthe mid 60s, and temperatures topping out in the low to mid 80s. \nHigh pressure remains over the area into early next week keeping rain\naway. Temperatures will gradually warm each day from Sunday through \nthe middle of next week as southerly flow returns.",
